  "reason": "The records collectively establish several relevant principles: water-cooled-hearth arc melting and quenching can retain nonequilibrium multiphase HEA structures; composition changes can produce chemically segregated dual phases; antiferromagnetic elements can suppress saturation magnetization; and heterogeneous magnetic phases, interfaces, anisotropy, vortices, or domain-wall interactions can raise coercivity. R1 is especially close to the processing intervention in a Fe-Co-Ni-based alloy, while R5, R10, R15, and R16 provide related magnetic-mechanism guidance. However, none identifies FeCoNiTi, an fcc/C14 Laves coexistence, Ti-sublattice antiferromagnetic coupling, the phase-specific 168/214/1084 K transitions, or the use of a compositionally matched C14-dominant comparator. Thus the literature points in the right direction, but selecting this exact alloy, stabilizing its particular fcc/C14 state, and assigning the high coercivity and transitions would require a substantial new idea."
